WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:06.540
A new ore. Lighter than silk. Harder that iron –&nbsp;
as weaponry, it would best the dwarves’ proudest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:12.120
blades. What is Durin talking about? Find out&nbsp;
about the origin story Tolkien never envisioned.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.140
Season 1 of "The Lord of the Rings: The&nbsp;
Rings of Power" has set out to expand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:20.460
J.R.R. Tolkien's lore rather than&nbsp;
adapt it beat-for-beat. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:24.540
this was always inevitable as the Amazon&nbsp;
series takes place during the Second Age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:28.680
of Middle-Earth, which isn't covered in&nbsp;
great detail in the author's writings. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.520
the show's writers have been tasked with&nbsp;
filling in the blanks in an attempt to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.780
a fully-realized universe that complements&nbsp;
the "Lord of the Rings" books and movies.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:42.120
As such, "The Rings of Power" has also been&nbsp;
an unexpected journey at times. The show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:45.720
creators have introduced some of their&nbsp;
own ideas into the proceedings as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:49.800
some of which cannot be found in any previous&nbsp;
"Lord of the Rings" story. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.240
Adar will upset Tolkien purists as he's&nbsp;
a brand new character who was created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:58.740
for the Amazon series. Furthermore, he's&nbsp;
nothing like the Elves in Tolkien's stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:03.300
though his existence indicates that "Rings of&nbsp;
Power" fans can look forward to a few surprises.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.960
The fifth installment of "The Rings of&nbsp;
Power" picks up the pace and continues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.120
the trend of adding fresh elements into the mix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.960
including one new origin story that&nbsp;
doesn't exist in Tolkien's writings.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.220
Before we get into how "The Rings of&nbsp;
Power" reimagines the origins of mithril,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:21.780
let's discuss its history in Tolkien's stories.&nbsp;
The "Lord of the Rings" books describe mithril&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.520
as a silvery metal that was primarily found&nbsp;
in the Mines of Moria. The material is both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.420
lightweight and strong, which makes it&nbsp;
ideal for creating battle armor. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.680
it also boasts a shiny glow that makes it&nbsp;
perfect for creating beautiful jewelry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:38.220
meaning that it was often craved by&nbsp;
those who enjoy the finer things in life.

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:41.940
Mithril is viewed as a source of&nbsp;
wealth and power in Middle-earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:46.920
having been worn by some of its most notable&nbsp;
figures. For example, Galadriel's ring, Nenya,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.340
was made of mithril and that's one of&nbsp;
the three Rings of Power that was given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:54.300
to the Elves. Expect that ring to factor into&nbsp;
"The Rings of Power" sometime in the future.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:59.580
Of course, any treasure that's so widely craved&nbsp;
brings out the worst in people. Mithril was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:04.380
main source of the Dwarves' wealth before their&nbsp;
kingdom was overtaken by the Balrog. Basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:08.280
the Dwarves became greedy and craved&nbsp;
more mithril, causing them to dig deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:12.000
into the mines and disrupt the fiery demon&nbsp;
that dwelled in the shadows. Naturally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.240
the demon wasn't willing to share it with&nbsp;
anyone who entered the mines afterward.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.780
Episode 5 of "The Rings of Power"&nbsp;
sees Gil-galad provide some fresh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:22.380
insight into the history of mithril.&nbsp;
The Elf connects it to the Silmarils,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:26.340
which are gems forged by the elf&nbsp;
Fëanor from the Trees of Valinor.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:30.900
During the episode, Gil-galad tells Elrond&nbsp;
about a legend pertaining to a pure-hearted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:36.180
Elven warrior who got into a scuffle with the&nbsp;
Balrog over a tree in the Misty Mountains. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:40.380
Elf poured all of his good into the tree&nbsp;
to protect it. The Balrog, meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:45.180
tried to destroy the tree and instilled it with&nbsp;
his evil powers. Their combined might caused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:49.800
lightning to strike, and their powers combined&nbsp;
to create mithril. According to Gil-galad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.240
the metal is, quote, "as pure and light as&nbsp;
good, as strong and unyielding as evil."

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.620
Mithril will now play an important role in&nbsp;
"The Rings of Power" as Gil-galad believes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:03.600
it's needed by spring to prevent the Elves from&nbsp;
experiencing a blight in Middle-earth. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:09.180
Elrond must convince Durin to give the elves some&nbsp;
mithril and save the day. Tolkien didn't associate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:13.920
mithril with the Silmarils, nor did he have&nbsp;
the Elves require the light to stay fresh. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:18.360
we can chalk this backstory up as another example&nbsp;
of "The Rings of Power" being unpredictable.

